P1-6  RESULTS.md L202 — Hall 2010 table row: internal convention inconsistency
      plus unjustified cross-metric numeric identification and ordering.
      Quotes: "Bound S ≤ min{2+3M, 4} = min{2+6F, 4}" (same row defines F as
      the RETAINED fraction, F = 1 − M/2); "In my metric Hall's S=4 model would
      be F = M/2 = 1/3"; "I reach S = 4 at F = 1/4 (cheaper), and S = 2√2 at
      F = (√2−1)/4 ≈ 0.104 (Hall's comparable cost is ≈ 0.276 in M, i.e. a
      larger 'distance')".
      Problems: (i) With F_ret = 1 − M/2, Hall's bound reads S ≤ min{8 − 6F_ret,
      4}, not min{2+6F, 4} — the latter is correct only for F = M/2 (fraction
      GIVEN UP). The row as written mixes the two conventions. (ii) "Hall's
      S=4 model would be F = M/2 = 1/3 in my metric" does not follow: our F is
      TV from a fixed uniform baseline; Hall's M is pairwise distance between
      setting-pair sources. No identity holds without the explicit source
      distributions. (iii) "cheaper" / "larger distance" order values across
      incommensurable metrics — exactly what literature_check.out §C forbids
      ("different metrics, different answers — do not present ours as
      contradicting theirs").
      Fix: state the three similar numbers side by side with their conventions
      and no ordering claim: Hall L1 convention M_singlet = 2(√2−1)/3 ≈ 0.276;
      fraction-of-MI-lost convention (√2−1)/3 ≈ 0.1381 (Hall's S=2√2 cost in
      that convention, and Alai's η=1 floor); our F* = (√2−1)/4 ≈ 0.1036 in the
      per-block-TV metric. "The metrics are incommensurable; no ordering of the
      numbers follows." Also fix (i): write min{8−6F, 4} under the retained-
      fraction label, or relabel F as M/2.

