SECTION C — PUBLICATION STRUCTURE & VENUE

C.1  Recommended outline (main text ~8–10 pp + supplement ~5 pp, PRA style).
     Main text:
       1. Introduction and motivation: the fine-tuning question; place inside
          the Hall-2010 / FGHK / Hall-Branciard "quantify measurement
          dependence" program; one paragraph on BHL 2026's "signalling in
          principle" as the motivation for the NS sub-case (with the P2-10
          distinction).
       2. Model and metric: finite model; F = max_ab TV(p(·|ab)‖ρ); state that
          ρ is arbitrary (the lower bound does not use uniformity);
          determinism WLOG for fixed p in one line (multi-affine ⇒ vertex, so
          stochastic responses give no advantage at fixed p).
       3. Lemma 1 + Theorem 1 (the engine) — short; the per-row TV optimum is
          the only non-combinatorial ingredient.
       4. Consequences: Corollary S ≤ 2+8F INCLUDING the one-line general-
          measure extension (fixes P1-1); Theorem 2 cap ⌈N/4⌉/N; Corollary 2
          headline F* = (√2−1)/min(N,4) with the N=4-saturation discussion
          ("the four correlators, not the source size, are the bottleneck").
       5. NS sub-case: Theorem 4 (N=2 flatness; statement + certificate
          pointer) and Theorem 5 (NS-silence for N≥3). State the explicit
          witness family in full — it is a highlight, not boilerplate; keep
          the gauge caveat.
       6. Explicit N=4 witness table (the concrete model a reader can check in
          30 seconds; cite audit1 Part D for the exact Q(√2) identity).
       7. Comparison with prior work: metric-convention table (Hall M / Hall
          F_ret / Alai η=1 floor / Pal δ_ens / our F) with the three numbers
          0.276 / 0.1381 / 0.1036 disambiguated and NO cross-metric ordering;
          citations: Hall 2010 (PRL 105, 250404), Hall & Branciard 2020
          (arXiv:2007.11903), Friedman–Guth–Hall–Kaiser–Gallicchio (PRA 99,
          012121 (2019)), BHL 2026 (arXiv:2602.11300), Alai arXiv:2608.18886 +
          arXiv:2608.00124, Pal et al. arXiv:2601.08290, Takakura et al.
          arXiv:2208.13634.
       8. Honest novelty paragraph with the literature_check §C hedge ("no
          prior use found in arXiv-indexed literature through 2026-08-23") and
          its stated coverage limits; keep the explicit not-a-breakthrough
          sentence.
     Supplement:
       - Theorem 3 exact curves N=2..8 + audit1.py certificate description
         (multiset enumeration, per-cell upper hull, Fraction arithmetic);
       - Theorem 4 certificate details (union-find over 256 vertices; 1312
         affine candidates; endpoint check);
       - full verification of the Theorem 5 row/NS identities and feasibility
         case check;
       - numerical cross-check record (verify.py, capregion.out,
         ns_tight_N{3,4}.out, ns_witness.out) + bisection-history artifact
         explanation; code/data availability statement.

C.2  Venue recommendation: Physical Review A (first choice).
     Reasoning: this is exactly where the "relaxed Bell inequalities / quantify
     measurement dependence" program has been published — Hall PRA 84, 022102
     (2011); Friedman et al. PRA 99, 012121 (2019); Supic–Bancal–Brunner PRA
     108, 042207 (2023). The result set (proved theorems + explicit
     constructions + exact-arithmetic certificates) matches PRA's taste, and a
     PRA referee will know Hall 2010 and FGHK by heart, so the comparison
     section can be direct. Alternatives: Foundations of Physics if the authors
     want to lean into the superdeterminism / NS-silence framing (BHL's
     audience; the "fine-tuning is NS-silent" message lands better there);
     Quantum (open access) if code availability and reproducibility are to be
     emphasized. PRD is acceptable but foundations content usually routes to
     PRA/FPH; JPA has a thinner foundations audience. A PRL letter is NOT
     recommended: the package (curves, cap, two NS theorems, witnesses) is too
     broad for a letter, and Hall's PRL was a single sharp bound.
     Expected length: 8–10 pages main text + 4–6 pages supplement.

C.3  What a referee at that venue will attack first (ranked):
     1. Metric choice: why TV from a fixed uniform baseline rather than Hall's
        baseline-free pairwise M? Pre-empt with the literature_check §C
        argument (the uniform source IS the MI assumption, so F measures
        per-setting deviation from MI directly) AND state that the lower bound
        S ≤ 2+8F holds for ANY ρ — the floor is a statement about all local
        models, not just uniform-source ones.
     2. Novelty positioning vs Alai arXiv:2608.18886 (appeared weeks before
        submission; "exact minimum MD for CHSH" in a Hall-type metric with
        Q(√2) certificates). Must cite both Alai papers and state clearly:
        different metric, different model class (Alai includes detector
        inefficiency/η_eff), different numbers ((√2−1)/3 vs our (√2−1)/4) — no
        contradiction.
     3. Model-class questions: (a) deterministic + uniform source — answer:
        determinism WLOG for fixed p (vertex argument); uniformity not needed
        for the lower bound; (b) do non-uniform ρ or stochastic responses
        change F*? — no, by the same two lines; state explicitly.
     4. NS-silence vs BHL: a foundations-literate referee will check that
        "NS-silent" (observable marginals) is not conflated with BHL's causal
        "signalling in principle". Keep the distinction sharp (P2-10).
     5. Minor: the N=2 "local polytope" interpretation (delete, P1-4); exact
        curves for N ≥ 9 left to numerics (fine if stated as PROOFS §8 does).

================================================================================
SECTION D — HONEST VERDICT

D.1  Classification: a REAL, publishable contribution — incremental-to-solid,
     NOT a breakthrough. Not "not publishable": after the P0/P1 fixes below it
     is a clean, self-contained package of proved theorems with exact
     certificates; but it does not open a new direction.
     Reasoning against literature_check.out: the per-block-TV-from-a-fixed-
     source metric has no prior use found in arXiv-indexed literature through
     2026-08-23 (sweep §C, Q1); the exact closed forms F* = (√2−1)/min(N,4)
     with N=4 saturation, the exact curves for N=2..8, the cap ⌈N/4⌉/N, and —
     most distinctively — the NS-silence theorems (F*_NS(N) = F*(N) for all
     N ≥ 3 with explicit algebraic witnesses at every N, plus the N=2 flatness
     theorem) do not appear in any swept work. But the closest prior work —
     Alai arXiv:2608.18886 (exact minimum MD for CHSH at 2√2, Hall-type metric,
     Q(√2) certificates, η_eff degrees of freedom) and Pal et al.
     arXiv:2601.08290 (first TV-based Bell fine-tuning bound found, |S| ≤
     2+6·δ_ens, drift mechanism, not claimed tight) — shows that "exact minimum
     measurement dependence for CHSH" is an ACTIVE 2026 front. The general
     program is no longer ours to claim; our contribution is a specific metric
     with clean closed forms plus the NS sub-case, which nothing in the sweep
     covers. What keeps it above trivial: (i) proved theorems with exact-
     arithmetic certificates, not numerics; (ii) the NS-silence result answers
     a question BHL 2026 raises only qualitatively, with explicit witnesses for
     every N ≥ 3 — the most defensibly new piece; (iii) the N=4 saturation is a
     clean structural fact. What caps it: the metric is one of several
     reasonable choices and must be argued for; the headline 0.1036 sits in a
     cluster of confusable numbers (0.276, 0.1381); two near-identical-spirit
     papers appeared within ~6 weeks of the sweep date.

D.2  Single strongest defensible contribution statement:
     "In the per-setting total-variation metric F = max_ab TV(p(·|ab)‖ρ), the
     minimum fine-tuning for a local deterministic hidden-variable model to
     reach the maximal quantum CHSH value S = 2√2 is exactly (√2−1)/min(N,4) —
     decreasing in the number N of hidden states and saturating at N = 4 — and
     this minimum is achievable without any observable signalling for every
     N ≥ 3 (while N = 2 admits no Bell violation under no-signalling at any
     fine-tuning level). To our knowledge — arXiv-indexed literature through
     2026-08-23, sweep described in the supplement — this metric and these
     exact values have no prior use."

D.3  Standing-commitment check.
     - Checkable without trusting prose: every headline number has an artifact
       — F*: audit1.out Part B + verify.py (D); curves: audit1.out Part A +
       capregion.out; cap: audit1.out Part A; N=4 witness: verify.py (A) +
       audit1.out Part D (exact Q(√2)); NS N=2: audit1.out Part E; NS N≥3:
       ns_general.out Parts A–C + ns_tight_N{3,4}.out + ns_witness.out. ONE
       GAP: RESULTS.md §7 does not yet point at audit1.py / ns_general.py
       (P1-9) — fix before submission.
     - Honest novelty: after the P1-7/P1-8 fixes the novelty language matches
       literature_check §C. No sentence currently drifts toward "breakthrough"
       (L6/L254 explicitly disclaim it — keep those sentences).
     - Numerically right but not proved: exactly ONE — the general-measure
       (infinite λ) form of S ≤ 2+8F asserted at RESULTS L161–164 (true; one-
       line proof; not written) — P1-1. Every other claim stated as exact is
       either proved in PROOFS.md or certified by audit1.py / ns_general.py.

